   "plaque": "arts_poets_corner",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Poets' Corner",
   "text": "Geoffrey Chaucer was buried in the south transept of Westminster Abbey in 1400, and when Edmund Spenser was laid beside him in 1599 a habit began. Writers have gathered here ever since: Charles Dickens in 1870, Thomas Hardy's ashes in 1928, and memorials to many more, from Shakespeare to a stone for Dylan Thomas in 1982. Burial is a high bar these days; most arrive as a plaque, a window or a floor stone, and the queue is very long and very quiet."
  }
